                          66 --27: /* PRQA S 0303 ++ # D001 Cast for accessing the area of register address */
                          67 --28: /* PRQA S 0306 ++ # D002 Cast for accessing the area of PKCCRAM address */
                          68 --29: /* PRQA S 0306 ++ # D003 Cast to use address as an integer value */
                          69 --30: /* PRQA S 0310, 3305 ++ # D004 Processing is done in units of WORD to increase processing speed. */
                          70 --31: /* PRQA S 0488 ++ # D007 Consider not to overrun the size of the array pointed to by the pointer.*/
                          71 --32: /* PRQA S 0751 ++ # D011 In order to allocate the buffer at the 4-byte boundary,
                          72 --33:                     the buffer defined by uint32_t type is cast to uint8_t type.*/
                          73 --34: /* PRQA S 1503 ++ # D013 No problem if it is unused because it is an API called from outside the library */
                          74 --35: /* PRQA S 1505, 1532 ++ # D014 Since files are grouped by function, they are called from different files. */
                          75 --36: /* PRQA S 2843 ++ # D017 No problem because it does not go through the path that refers to the invalid pointer */
                          76 --37: /* PRQA S 2953 ++ # D019 A calculation required to swap and set the value in PKCCRAM,
                          77 --38:                         and there is no problem because it is intentionally  subtracted. */
                          78 --39: /* PRQA S 2981 ++ # D020 Local variable ret of APIs sets INTERNALERR to the initial value
                          79 --40:                         as a countermeasure against attacks that pass through processing. */
                          80 --41: /* PRQA S 3383, 3384 ++ # D018 Have confirmed in advance that the value doesn't wrap around. */
                          81 --42: /* PRQA S 3416 ++ # D025 Because it is volatile to prevent the timeout count from being optimized. */
                          82 --43: /* PRQA S 3469 ++ # D026 If it is a function, overhead is large, so it corresponds with macro. */
                          83 --44: /* PRQA S 5002 ++ # D030 There is no problem because it is used in a system that can distinguish
                          84 --45:                     file names of 8 characters or more. */
